   "text": {
    "L1": "A matka keeps water cool, but the clay is not cold by itself.",
    "L2": "The cooling comes from evaporation at the matka's outer surface.",
    "L3": "Water inside seeps through the clay's tiny pores to the outside.",
    "L4": "That outer water evaporates into the air and takes heat from the pot and water inside.",
    "L5": "So the water cools. This works best when the air is hot, dry and moving.",
    "L6": "A fan cools sweaty skin by speeding up sweat evaporation, not by making the air colder.",
    "L7": "Evaporation is also faster when water is hotter or spread over a larger surface.",
    "L8": "To test one factor fairly, change only that factor and keep everything else the same."
   },
